(project convention-go
 (doc "go-standard convention: structural rules and code intelligence
       for Go projects using borge. Analogous to how ocaml-dune is
       the implicit convention for borge's own codebase.")
 (status planned)
 (no-inline)
 (section convention-definition
  (doc "What (convention go-standard) declares about project structure")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        When a root .borg file declares (convention go-standard), the
        following structural rules apply:

        DIRECTORY LAYOUT:
        - cmd/ owns executable entrypoints (one dir per binary)
        - internal/ owns private packages (not importable externally)
        - pkg/ owns public library packages (importable by external)
        - api/ owns API definitions (protobuf, OpenAPI, etc.)
        - configs/ owns configuration files
        - test/ or *_test.go files own integration tests
        - go.mod at project root defines the module path

        PACKAGE BOUNDARIES:
        - Each directory is a package; package name = dir name
        - Capitalized identifiers are exported (Go convention)
        - internal/ packages enforce privacy at compile time

        BUILD:
        - go build ./... — compile all packages
        - go test ./... — run all tests
        - go vet ./... — static analysis
        - No dune files, no separate build system

        DOC COMMENTS:
        - Go's // and /* */ doc comments above declarations are
          the native format. golint / go vet enforce doc on exported
          declarations.
        - Borge annotated comments in Go nest the standard (* *)
          grammar inside Go's /* */ block comment delimiters:
            OCaml:  (* author note (|description|) *)
            Go:     /* (* author note (|description|) *) */
          The inner (* *) is the same borge grammar that Doc_detect,
          Borg_comment, and all existing code already understands.
          No new parsing code needed for comment content.

        DRIFT DETECTION:
        - Spec drift: same as OCaml — .borg section claims implemented
          but no corresponding code exists
        - Code drift: packages discovered from go.mod + directory
          structure vs sections in .borg files
        - Structural drift: domain logic in cmd/ when go-standard
          says pkg/ or internal/ owns it
        - Export drift: Go exported symbols (capitalized) vs what
          the spec describes

        COMMENT INTEGRITY:
        - Same rules as OCaml: agent-authored comments can be modified
          by agents; human-authored comments cannot be silently
          deleted. The nested delimiters do not affect semantics.
      |))
  )
 )
 (section comment-format
  (doc "Borge comment grammar in Go source files")
  (status planned)
  (details
   (doc (|
        THE KEY INSIGHT: Borge comments in Go nest the standard (* *)
        grammar inside Go's /* */ block comment delimiters. This means
        the existing Doc_detect, Borg_comment, and Doc_extract modules
        work unchanged — they already know how to find (* *) and parse
        the borge grammar inside. The outer /* */ is invisible to them.

        Why not /* */ as the borge delimiter? Because that would
        require duplicating the entire comment parsing pipeline:
        is_borg_note, parse_borg_note, is_borg_short, is_exempt_marker,
        extract_comment_content, the multi-line comment tracker in
        Doc_detect, etc. The nested approach trades a small visual
        cost for a large code savings: zero new comment parsing code.

        Supported forms in Go files:

        1. Annotated comment (full borge style):
           /* (* agent note (|
              WHAT: Parse incoming HTTP requests.
              WHY: Handlers need structured access to request data.
            |) *) */
           func ParseRequest(r *http.Request) (*Request, error) { ... }

           The outer /* */ is Go's block comment. The inner (* *) is
           the standard borge grammar. Doc_detect finds the (* *) and
           processes it exactly as it would in an OCaml file.

        2. Short borg comment:
           /* (*| Parse incoming HTTP requests. |*) */
           func ParseRequest(r *http.Request) (*Request, error) { ... }

        3. Exempt marker:
           /* (* exempt doc *) */
           func internalHelper(x int) int { ... }

        4. Drift marker inside a borge comment:
           /* (* agent note (|
              WHAT: Parse incoming HTTP requests.
              WHY: Handlers need structured access to request data.
            |) (status drifted) *) */

        5. Function-level borg form:
           /* (* (fn ParseRequest
                (doc "Parse incoming HTTP requests")
                (since "v0.1")) *) */
           func ParseRequest(r *http.Request) (*Request, error) { ... }

        6. Go doc comments (standard // above declaration):
           // ParseRequest parses an incoming HTTP request.
           func ParseRequest(r *http.Request) (*Request, error) { ... }

           These are standard Go doc comments. They are NOT borge
           comments — they cannot carry authorship or verbatim
           content. For doc coverage, they count as documented
           (GoDocstring) but lack the structured metadata that
           borge comments provide.

        What does NOT work in Go:
        - Line comments (//) cannot carry borge annotations because
          they are single-line and cannot span the (* *) delimiters
          or (|...|) verbatim syntax required for borge comments.
        - A bare /* (* ... */) */ without the outer /* */ is also
          valid (Go allows nested appearances in comments) but the
          convention is to always use the outer wrapper for clarity.

        NO NEW COMMENT PARSING MODULES NEEDED:
        Because borge comments use the same (* *) delimiters inside
        Go's /* */ wrapper, the following modules work unchanged:
        - Doc_detect — finds (* *) and classifies borge comments
        - Borg_comment — extracts (fn ...) forms from (* *)
        - Doc_extract — counts bindings for coverage
        - Doc_coverage — computes coverage the same way

        The only new modules are Go-specific:
        - Go_parse — discovers packages from go.mod + directories
        - Go_surface — extracts exported symbols from .go files
        - Go_doc_extract — extracts Go func/type/var/const bindings
      |))
  )
 )
 (section modules
  (doc "New modules needed for go-standard convention support")
  (subsection go-parse
   (doc "Parse go.mod and directory structure for package discovery")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Analogous to Dune_parse. Discovers Go project structure by
         parsing go.mod and walking the directory tree.

         Types:
         - go_module: module path from go.mod (e.g. "github.com/foo/bar")
         - go_package: directory path, package name, whether internal
         - go_executable: cmd/ subdirectory name
         - go_test: *_test.go files or test/ directory

         Functions:
         - parse_go_mod : string -> go_module option
         - discover_packages : string -> go_package list
         - discover_executables : string -> go_executable list
         - parse_all : string -> go_project
         - all_package_names : go_project -> string list
         - library_packages : go_project -> go_package list
         - executable_packages : go_project -> go_package list
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection go-surface
   (doc "Extract exported symbols from .go files")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Analogous to Surface. Extracts the public interface of a
         Go package by scanning .go files for exported declarations.

         Go export rule: a name starting with an uppercase letter
         is exported. This is enforced by the compiler — no .mli
         equivalent needed.

         Types:
         - go_symbol_kind: Func | Method | Type | Var | Const
         - go_symbol: { name; kind; file; line; is_exported }
         - go_package_surface: { path; package_name; symbols; source }

         Functions:
         - is_exported_name : string -> bool
         - extract_func_decl : string -> (string * go_symbol_kind) option
         - is_test_or_benchmark : string -> bool
         - extract_type_decl : string -> string option
         - extract_var_or_const_decl -> string option
         - extract_file_surface : string -> go_symbol list
         - find_go_files : string -> string list
         - extract_package_surface : string -> go_package_surface
         - exported_names : go_package_surface -> string list
         - all_names : go_package_surface -> string list
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection go-doc-extract
   (doc "Extract Go bindings for documentation coverage")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Analogous to Doc_extract. Extracts Go declaration bindings
         (func, type, var, const) for documentation coverage analysis.

         Types:
         - go_binding_info: { name; line; is_exported; is_test; is_internal }

         Functions:
         - extract_go_bindings : string -> go_binding_info list
         - find_go_files : string -> string list
         - extract_project_go_bindings : string -> (string * go_binding_info) list
         - count_stats : go_binding_info list -> int * int * int * int

         NOTE: Doc detection for Go files uses the EXISTING Doc_detect
         module unchanged. Because borge comments in Go nest the
         standard (* *) grammar inside Go's /* */ block comments,
         Doc_detect already knows how to find and classify them.
         There is no go_doc_detect module — it is not needed.

         Similarly, borge comments in Go files are extracted by the
         EXISTING Borg_comment module. There is no go_comment module.
       |))
   )
  )
 )
 (section convention-dispatch
  (doc "How borge selects the right convention at runtime")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Borge dispatches to the appropriate module set based on the
        convention declared in the root .borg file.

        Convention resolution:
        1. Read the root .borg file
        2. Extract the (convention NAME) form
        3. Dispatch to the appropriate module set:

        | Feature         | ocaml-dune        | go-standard         |
        |----------------|-------------------|---------------------|
        | Package parse  | Dune_parse        | Go_parse            |
        | Surface extr.  | Surface           | Go_surface           |
        | Binding extr.  | Doc_extract       | Go_doc_extract       |
        | Doc detection  | Doc_detect        | Doc_detect (same!)   |
        | Borg comments  | Borg_comment      | Borg_comment (same!) |
        | Code drift     | Drift (dune-aw.)  | Drift (go-mod-aware) |
        | Borge comment  | (* ... *) inside   (* ... *) inside     |
        |                | .ml file          | /* */ wrapper in .go |
        | Docstring      | (** ... *)        | // above decl        |
        | Exempt mark   | (* exempt doc *)   | /* (* exempt doc *) */|
        | File extension | .ml / .mli        | .go                  |
        | Excl. dirs    | _build/           | vendor/              |

        Notice: Doc_detect and Borg_comment are SHARED between
        conventions. This is the benefit of the nested comment
        approach — no duplicate parsing code.

        Implementation:
        - Convention.t type (variant: Ocaml_dune | Go_standard)
        - Convention.resolve : string -> t
        - Each command dispatches via Convention

        Fallback:
        - If no (convention ...) form exists, assume ocaml-dune.
        - If convention is unknown, warn and use ocaml-dune as default.
       |))
  )
 )
)
